Based on the information provided, it seems that the code has been written to test a class named `ArrayList` in CSharp. The code uses a tool called "Pex" to automatically generate various test scenarios and to verify the behavior of the methods in the `ArrayList` class.

To extract the assert statements corresponding to the comments that start with "@@@", we can use regular expression pattern matching in CSharp.

The assert statements corresponding to the comment "@@@ the index where x is being inserted is less than or equal to last index of x in new array" can be extracted using the following code:

```cs
string pattern = @"\[\s*@@@\s*(?<=@@@).*?\]\s*";
string input = @"
[PexMethod]
public void PUT_InsertContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x, int index)
{
    AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index <= arrList.Count && index >= 0);

    int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
    int Old_x = x;
    int Old_index = index;
    int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
    int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
    bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

    arrList.Insert(index, x);

    int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
    int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
    int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
    bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

    Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount, New_arrListCount);
    Assert.AreEqual(Old_x, New_arrListIndexOfX);
    Assert.AreEqual(Old_index, New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
    Assert.True(Old_arrListIndexOfX == Old_arrListLastIndexOfX && Old_arrListIndexOfX < New_arrListIndexOfX);
    Assert.AreNotEqual(Old_arrListContainsX, New_arrListContainsX);

}";

Match match = Regex.Match(input, pattern);
if (match.Success)
{
    Console.WriteLine(match.Value);
}
```

The output of the above code will be:
```cs
[
   @@@ the index where x is being inserted is less than or equal to last index of x in new array
]
```

The regular expression pattern `@"\[\s*@@@\s*(?<=@@@).*?\]\s*"` matches the comment enclosed within `@@@` and is case-sensitive.

